# Rust Typestate Example

This rule demonstrates concrete implementations of the Typestate pattern in Rust to illustrate best practices.

## Example: Document Processing System

Consider a document processing system that must go through specific states:

1. `Draft` - Initial document creation
2. `PendingReview` - Submitted for review
3. `Published` - Approved and published

```rust
// The state types - each is a separate struct
struct Draft {
    content: String,
}

struct PendingReview {
    content: String,
}

struct Published {
    content: String,
}

// Implementation for the Draft state
impl Draft {
    // Constructor for the initial state
    pub fn new(content: String) -> Self {
        Self { content }
    }

    // State transition to PendingReview
    pub fn request_review(self) -> PendingReview {
        PendingReview { content: self.content }
    }

    // Method only available in the Draft state
    pub fn add_content(&mut self, text: &str) {
        self.content.push_str(text);
    }
}

// Implementation for the PendingReview state
impl PendingReview {
    // State transition to Draft (rejection)
    pub fn reject(self) -> Draft {
        Draft { content: self.content }
    }

    // State transition to Published
    pub fn approve(self) -> Published {
        Published { content: self.content }
    }

    // No editing methods available in PendingReview state!
}

// Implementation for the Published state
impl Published {
    // Methods specific to published documents
    pub fn content(&self) -> &str {
        &self.content
    }

    // No state transitions from Published in this example
}
```

## Typestate with Generic Parameter

For more complex scenarios, you can use a generic parameter to represent the state:

```rust
// State marker types (zero-sized types)
struct Unopened;
struct Opened;
struct Closed;

// The file with a generic state parameter
struct File<State> {
    name: String,
    data: Vec<u8>,
    state: std::marker::PhantomData<State>,
}

// Initial state implementation
impl File<Unopened> {
    pub fn new(name: String) -> Self {
        Self {
            name,
            data: Vec::new(),
            state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }

    pub fn open(self) -> File<Opened> {
        File {
            name: self.name,
            data: self.data,
            state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

// Opened state implementation
impl File<Opened> {
    pub fn write(&mut self, data: &[u8]) {
        self.data.extend_from_slice(data);
    }

    pub fn close(self) -> File<Closed> {
        File {
            name: self.name,
            data: self.data,
            state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

// Closed state implementation
impl File<Closed> {
    pub fn delete(self) -> () {
        // Clean up resources
    }
}
```

## Combining Newtype and Typestate Patterns

This example demonstrates combining both patterns for a measurement validation system:

```rust
// Newtype pattern for units of measurement
struct Celsius(f64);
struct Fahrenheit(f64);
struct Validated;
struct Unvalidated;

// Combining with typestate for temperature reading validation
struct TemperatureReading<Unit, State> {
    value: Unit,
    timestamp: u64,
    _state: std::marker::PhantomData<State>,
}

// Unvalidated Celsius reading
impl TemperatureReading<Celsius, Unvalidated> {
    pub fn new_celsius(value: f64) -> Self {
        Self {
            value: Celsius(value),
            timestamp: std::time::SystemTime::now()
                .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
                .unwrap()
                .as_secs(),
            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }

    pub fn validate(self) -> Result<TemperatureReading<Celsius, Validated>, ValidationError> {
        // Celsius must be between -273.15 and 1000 for this system
        if self.value.0 >= -273.15 && self.value.0 <= 1000.0 {
            Ok(TemperatureReading {
                value: self.value,
                timestamp: self.timestamp,
                _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
            })
        } else {
            Err(ValidationError::OutOfRange)
        }
    }

    pub fn to_fahrenheit(self) -> TemperatureReading<Fahrenheit, Unvalidated> {
        TemperatureReading {
            value: Fahrenheit(self.value.0 * 9.0/5.0 + 32.0),
            timestamp: self.timestamp,
            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

// Unvalidated Fahrenheit reading
impl TemperatureReading<Fahrenheit, Unvalidated> {
    pub fn new_fahrenheit(value: f64) -> Self {
        Self {
            value: Fahrenheit(value),
            timestamp: std::time::SystemTime::now()
                .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
                .unwrap()
                .as_secs(),
            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }

    pub fn validate(self) -> Result<TemperatureReading<Fahrenheit, Validated>, ValidationError> {
        // Fahrenheit must be between -459.67 and 1832 for this system
        if self.value.0 >= -459.67 && self.value.0 <= 1832.0 {
            Ok(TemperatureReading {
                value: self.value,
                timestamp: self.timestamp,
                _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
            })
        } else {
            Err(ValidationError::OutOfRange)
        }
    }

    pub fn to_celsius(self) -> TemperatureReading<Celsius, Unvalidated> {
        TemperatureReading {
            value: Celsius((self.value.0 - 32.0) * 5.0/9.0),
            timestamp: self.timestamp,
            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

// The validated readings can be processed
impl<Unit> TemperatureReading<Unit, Validated> {
    pub fn process(&self) -> ProcessedResult {
        // Process the validated temperature reading
        ProcessedResult {}
    }

    pub fn timestamp(&self) -> u64 {
        self.timestamp
    }
}

// Error type
enum ValidationError {
    OutOfRange,
}

// Result of processing
struct ProcessedResult {}

// Example usage:
fn main() {
    // Create an unvalidated Celsius reading
    let reading = TemperatureReading::new_celsius(25.0);

    // Convert to Fahrenheit (still unvalidated)
    let fahrenheit_reading = reading.to_fahrenheit();

    // Validate the Fahrenheit reading
    match fahrenheit_reading.validate() {
        Ok(validated) => {
            // Only validated readings can be processed
            let result = validated.process();
            println!("Processed at timestamp: {}", validated.timestamp());
        }
        Err(_) => println!("Validation failed"),
    }

    // This won't compile - can't call process() on unvalidated reading
    // let invalid = TemperatureReading::new_celsius(5000.0).process();
}
```

This combined example demonstrates:

1. Newtypes (`Celsius`, `Fahrenheit`) for type-safe units of measurement
2. Typestate (`Unvalidated`, `Validated`) to enforce validation before processing
3. Methods available only in specific states
4. Type safety preventing mixing different units
5. Compile-time enforcement of the validation workflow
6. Zero runtime overhead: The state tracking via PhantomData has no runtime cost
7. Self-documenting: The API guides users through the correct sequence of operations

Use these patterns to implement type-safe state machines in your Rust code.

## Key implementation patterns to note

1. **Ownership transfer**: Each state transition takes `self` by value, consuming the original state
2. **State-specific methods**: Methods are only available on the appropriate state
3. **No invalid transitions**: The compiler prevents invalid operations (e.g., writing to a closed file)
4. **Clean API**: Users can only follow the defined workflows
5. **Type safety**: Errors are caught at compile time, not runtime
6. **Zero runtime overhead**: The state tracking via PhantomData has no runtime cost
7. **Self-documenting**: The API guides users through the correct sequence of operations

Use these patterns to implement type-safe state machines in your Rust code.
